div.p:
After meeting and uniting on account of previous action,This most likely refers to actions in their past lives, in which case this is an allusion to the fact that their relationship is divinely ordained. Or, it might be an elliptical reference to the ‘prologue’ mentioned in the apparatus notes. the things called [the hero] expressing desire; 
        [the hero] making clear [his desire]; [the hero’s] fear of separation; [the hero’s] encouragement [of her];
        [the hero’s] difficulty obtaining [her]; [the hero] saying, ‘[She is like my] life’; and [the hero feeling] comforted then
        departing – these are the divine union.
